WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.060 --> 00:00:07.300
All of the television programs and all
the things we'd done were all very important.

00:00:07.300 --> 00:00:13.360
But what with the urgency of
the landing on the moon

00:00:13.460 --> 00:00:16.980
everything was checked and 
checked and checked again

00:00:17.120 --> 00:00:23.740
because if we'd messed up I mean there would have been a lot of people very, very disappointed.

00:00:23.820 --> 00:00:29.360
And when you look back now and realize there was 220 million people in Europe watching it.

00:00:29.369 --> 00:00:35.850
Goonhilly had done a lot of the broadcasts from America showing the launches of various

00:00:35.850 --> 00:00:42.480
vehicles that went into space from
Mercury, Gemini, right up to the Apollo series.

00:00:42.900 --> 00:00:46.340
With the Apollo series they
showed all the launches because

00:00:46.340 --> 00:00:49.060
everybody was very interested.

00:00:49.060 --> 00:00:50.780
'Liftoff on Apollo 11.'

00:00:52.760 --> 00:00:57.160
When they launched on the Tuesday

00:00:57.160 --> 00:00:58.600
away they went

00:00:59.840 --> 00:01:03.600
and you think well this is going to
happen now

00:01:03.600 --> 00:01:09.990
and because we'd seen so many of the
launches so many we almost felt involved

00:01:10.000 --> 00:01:11.880
with the project.

00:01:11.880 --> 00:01:17.280
And when finally they
got to the moon, they went into the lunar

00:01:17.280 --> 00:01:23.760
orbit and they broke free and went down

00:01:23.760 --> 00:01:25.520
I mean the atmosphere in the old

00:01:25.520 --> 00:01:29.800
operation control area was electric, 
it was electric.

00:01:29.800 --> 00:01:31.520
Nobody spoke.

00:01:31.520 --> 00:01:33.680
There was no need to speak.

00:01:33.680 --> 00:01:38.360
Because if somebody had
spoken I think they were broken the spell.

00:01:38.360 --> 00:01:42.360
Finally, when they opened the hatch 
and they came down

00:01:42.360 --> 00:01:47.850
we were almost walking down there a step
at the time with him because we've been

00:01:47.850 --> 00:01:50.800
involved, we felt part of it.

00:01:51.200 --> 00:01:56.720
And when they finally took off and they

00:01:56.759 --> 00:02:03.030
joined with the command module and
literally it was all over.

00:02:03.040 --> 00:02:05.200
It was a wonderful, wonderful experience.

00:02:05.200 --> 00:02:10.880
I can't really explain exactly what it was like.

00:02:10.880 --> 00:02:16.459
When I left in the morning I walked out
the door and if someone had said to me

00:02:16.480 --> 00:02:21.760
'You'll be back here in 50 years time to
talk about it' I wouldn't have believed it.

00:02:21.760 --> 00:02:26.070
It was two very important things that
went on, the landing on the moon

00:02:26.080 --> 00:02:28.080
and Goonhilly handling the broadcast.

00:02:28.080 --> 00:02:30.959
My responsibility on the night of the

00:02:30.959 --> 00:02:35.360
broadcast I was in charge of the
television switching, patching from the

00:02:35.360 --> 00:02:41.459
communication equipment through the various feeds and then the outlet which

00:02:41.459 --> 00:02:47.600
would go via the microwave link up to
BBC for Europe or for the UK.

00:02:47.760 --> 00:02:53.579
Now, a lot of people thought that the pictures from the moon came directly to Goonhilly,

00:02:53.579 --> 00:02:54.962
but they didn't.

00:02:54.962 --> 00:03:00.150
They came eventually,
because of a satellite failure over the

00:03:00.160 --> 00:03:06.320
Atlantic ocean,
it came in by a very long route.

00:03:06.320 --> 00:03:19.920
It came from the moon to Australia, Australia to
America, America across to Japan,

00:03:19.920 --> 00:03:22.400
and Japan to Goonhilly.

00:03:22.428 --> 00:03:25.360
When I came here in the early 60's
there was only number one.

00:03:25.360 --> 00:03:28.480
Number two came in,

00:03:28.480 --> 00:03:32.880
they built in the late 60's, it
was just a modern version of number one

00:03:33.760 --> 00:03:39.750
Number 1, well they call it Arthur now,
it was built to a design which was

00:03:39.750 --> 00:03:41.520
typically British.

00:03:41.520 --> 00:03:44.835
It was built like a battleship.

00:03:44.835 --> 00:03:47.829
Aerial 1 weighs 1100 tons.

00:03:47.829 --> 00:03:50.474
I mean it is there forever.

00:03:50.474 --> 00:03:54.099
It had a character and even though it

00:03:54.099 --> 00:03:58.880
was built like a battleship it is the
Grand Old Lady of Goonhilly,

00:03:58.880 --> 00:04:01.360
and I just hope it'll always be here.

00:04:01.360 --> 00:04:05.840
Goonhilly was built on this site

00:04:06.000 --> 00:04:07.040
because it used to be

00:04:07.040 --> 00:04:10.480
a radar station during the war,

00:04:10.480 --> 00:04:13.090
but important
thing at the time when the satellite

00:04:13.090 --> 00:04:18.159
system started coming in they wanted to
be as far west as possible and as far

00:04:18.159 --> 00:04:25.780
south as possible and the best site
they found was on Goonhilly Downs.

00:04:25.780 --> 00:04:33.280
I came to Cornwall
in the early 1960s to work at Goonhilly.

00:04:33.280 --> 00:04:39.190
When I got here I looked around and thought
'have I made the right choice?'

00:04:39.190 --> 00:04:44.080
It was a bleak, dismal, windy place.

00:04:44.080 --> 00:04:46.720
I just didn't think that I made the right decision but

00:04:46.720 --> 00:04:51.360
I'm afraid 60 odd years later I did
make the right decision.

00:04:52.880 --> 00:04:54.880
I'd been part of it

00:04:55.120 --> 00:04:56.240
as far as I was concerned.

00:04:56.240 --> 00:04:59.840
I was there, I'd done it, and I think I 
achieved a great deal.

00:04:59.840 --> 00:05:03.840
And it's a legacy that will go on.

00:05:03.840 --> 00:05:05.600
My great-grandchildren say

00:05:05.600 --> 00:05:08.160
'hey, my granddad was involved with that.'

00:05:08.160 --> 00:05:11.020
To me that is a legacy and

00:05:11.040 --> 00:05:14.560
it was a great part of my working life at Goonhilly.

